# Update Rules

## Image Compression
- Every new image added to Wayfarer must be compressed and optimized before being included in the project.
- Prefer WebP for photographic or illustrated backgrounds, character art, and theme icons when transparency and visual quality are preserved. Use PNG when lossless transparency or other specific requirements make it necessary.
- Keep images visually clear at their actual display size while minimizing file size.
- Update every asset reference and the service-worker cache when adding, converting, renaming, or moving images.
- Verify that every referenced image exists and loads after the update.

- Any new picture/image added to Wayfarer must be compressed/optimized before inclusion.
- Every theme must include its own optimized full-screen app background, registered to the theme and displayed behind the interface with a legibility overlay.

Every new theme must be tested for its complete visual identity: dedicated background with overlay, theme-specific palette applied to major UI components, active body class, icon, character art, soundtrack, Store/Inventory/equip integration, and Debug test coverage.


## Theme display identity and visual regression enforcement
- Display names may be changed without changing stable internal theme IDs, skill names, unlock keys, saved equipment IDs, or quest reward keys.
- Debug theme compliance tests must verify actual browser-computed styles and the lower interface (including Inventory and equipment surfaces), not merely the existence of CSS text.
- Test theme background and icon HTTP responses and image decoding. Report each failure individually with expected/actual details and suggested investigation. Never report a theme as passed solely because its asset path or selector exists.
- When checking non-active themes, use temporary in-memory class changes only and restore the original body classes immediately; never mutate saved user progress during visual tests.


## Debug and recovery safeguards (v0.15.5)
- Theme/quest/store diagnostic tests and feature-isolation switches must be restricted to Admin while Sandbox mode is active.
- Dungeon is the starter theme and must not be expected in the unlockable Store catalog. Only the theme equipment slot is required to show theme-specific changes; empty artifact/pet slots may remain blank.
- Debug must clearly distinguish PASS, FAIL, and INCONCLUSIVE. A catalog/style presence check is not proof of end-to-end behavior.
- Character reset must require explicit confirmation, preserve Journal, To Do, and Groceries, and offer one-step restoration from a snapshot. Explain which character fields are reset before confirmation.
- XP/gold grant tools are Admin-only, can run in live or Sandbox, and must clearly state which environment is being changed.
- Store and quest tests must not alter real progress; simulated purchase/reward flows should run only against isolated sandbox state.


## Data-driven gameplay fault injection (v0.15.54)
- Debug unlock, purchase, and theme-bonus XP injectors must only operate for Admin inside Sandbox and must alter the actual shared gameplay decision/reward path. Tests must inspect observed results, never infer failure merely from a checked injector.
- Store integration tests must iterate every registered unlockable theme, simulate its required skill level and exact price in isolated state, and verify actual ownership and remaining currency after the purchase transaction.
- Theme definitions that grant a skill bonus must declare `perkSkill`; quest-generation tests discover skills from the skill catalog and theme perks from the unlockable registry. Skills with no registered theme perk must be marked INCONCLUSIVE for bonus testing rather than falsely passing.
- Quest XP tests must create an isolated eligible quest through the shared theme-bonus calculation and award XP through the same reward helper used by quest completion. Never mutate real quests, inventory, currency, or saved progress during diagnostic tests.
